 * =============================================================================
 * Application_View_to_UML.ajs
 * Version 3.0
 * =============================================================================
 *
 * Purpose
 * -------
 * Generates a UML-inspired component view from the selected  ArchiMate Application View. The output remains an
 * ArchiMate View  and is intended to provide a UML Component Diagram-style representation of application components and
 * their relationships.
 *
 * Version History
 * ---------------
 * V1.0: In version 1.0 it used the script used to operate in dual mode:
 *        1) Heuristic-only mode: uses static type mappings to create UML-like labels and stereotypes.
 *        2) AI-enhanced mode: calls a local Ollama-compatible REST endpoint through sendToAI.js to semantically analyze
 *           selected elements and enrich their documentation and stereotypes.
 *
 * V2.0: Unlike the previously non-AI version, the script, in version 2.0, uses Ollama (via sendToAI.js) for semantic
 *       analysis of each element to generate smarter UML-like stereotypes and enriched alternative ArchiMate views. The
 *       script is now fully integrated with Ollama and can gracefully degrade to heuristic-only mode if the Ollama
 *       service is unavailable. It also includes improved logging, and error handling.
 *
 * V3.0: The script now implements a 'derivative-style UML representation' approach, which classifies ArchiMate concepts
 *       by their semantic family (component, interface, service, behavior, data, event) rather than performing a direct
 *       1:1 type conversion. This allows for more accurate UML-like visualization while preserving ArchiMate semantics.
 *       The script also includes enhanced visual styling based on semantic families and relationship roles. A thorough
 *       research on the related concepts have been carried out on the white papers, dealing with the issue at hand,
 *       leading to these semantic and visual improvements, in the latest version.
 *
 * Intended Use
 * ------------
 * Typically run after selecting a source ArchiMate view such as:
 *   - Phase C - Information Systems Architecture
 *   - A business/application layer diagram
 *   - A view containing application components, data objects, and business
 *     objects connected by relationships
 *
 * The script reads the selected view, extracts supported concepts and relations, creates a new view named
 * "<Source View> (UML)", and places the extracted elements into a grid layout. It also adds relationships between
 * matching elements in the new view when possible.
 *
 * Supported Concept Types
 * -----------------------
 * The script recognizes and includes the following ArchiMate concept families:
 *
 *   - application-component
 *   - application-collaboration
 *   - application-interface
 *   - application-service
 *   - application-function
 *   - application-process
 *   - application-interaction
 *   - application-event
 *   - application-object
 *   - data-object
 *   - business-object
 *   - business-process
 *
 * Supported relationship types are mapped into UML-style connections, including:
 *   composition, aggregation, association, realization, generalization, serving, access, flow, triggering, assignment,
 *   and influence.
 *
 * Architecture and Workflow
 * -------------------------
 * 1) Select a source view from the current Archi selection.
 * 2) Extract all visual objects from that view.
 * 3) Keep only recognized application/business/data concepts.
 * 4) Create a new UML-style Archi view in the model.
 * 5) For each concept:
 *      - optionally call the local Ollama endpoint for semantic enrichment
 *      - sanitize the AI result if needed
 *      - store a stereotype or documentation note
 *      - add the concept to the new view at a calculated grid position
 * 6) Recreate supported connections between source and target concepts that
 *    exist in the new view.
 * 7) Print a summary report and display an alert with the result.
 *
 * AI Integration Details
 * ----------------------
 * The optional AI layer relies on sendToAI.js, which calls the local Ollama
 * API at:
 *   http://localhost:11434/v1/chat/completions
 *
 * The script sends a short, structured prompt asking the model to suggest a UML stereotype for the selected ArchiMate
 * concept. Example prompts include:
 *   - "Given an ArchiMate element named 'Policy Data' ... suggest a brief UML stereotype (1-2 words, lowercase) ..."
 *   - "Provide a brief UML-oriented description of an ArchiMate ..."
 *
 * Responses are cleaned to remove Unicode formatting characters, zero-width separators, and excessive whitespace before
 * being used. If the AI response is invalid, empty, or null, the script falls back gracefully to a heuristic stereotype.
 *
 * If Ollama returns HTTP errors repeatedly, the script automatically switches to heuristic-only mode.
 *
 * Output Markers
 * --------------
 * When AI is enabled, the generated element documentation is prefixed with a recognizable marker to distinguish
 * AI-enriched items from heuristic output.
 *
 * Examples:
 *   <<[AI] entity>>
 *   <<[AI] service>>
 *
 * Heuristic-only output is marked similarly:
 *   <<[HEUR] dataobject>>
 *
 * The generated view documentation also records whether the output is AI-enriched or heuristic-only.
 *
 * Important Behavior Notes
 * ------------------------
 * - The script expects the user to select the desired source view manually.
 * - It does not validate that the selected view is semantically correct; it
 *   simply converts whatever supported concepts it finds.
 * - It only creates diagram objects for recognized types.
 * - Unsupported relationship types are skipped and logged in the console.
 * - Connection creation depends on both endpoints being present in the newly
 *   generated view; if not, the link is skipped.
 * - AI analysis is optional: if Ollama is absent or the service fails, the script
 *   still generates a valid UML-like view using heuristics.
 *
 * File Dependencies
 * -----------------
 * Requires the following in the same directory or accessible runtime scope:
 *   - sendToAI.js  (optional but recommended for AI enrichment)
 *
 * Runtime Context
 * ---------------
 * This script is intended to run in Archi's JArchi/GraalJS environment, not as a
 * standard browser or Node.js script. It relies on the Archi model API, selection
 * objects, global load(), and Java interop capabilities available in the
 * JArchi/GraalJS runtime.
 *
 * Result
 * ------
 * The script creates a new Archi view in the model named: <Original View Name> (UML)
 *
 * The new view contains:
 *   - UML-like concept boxes for supported application/business/data elements
 *   - AI-generated or heuristic UML stereotype hints
 *   - A console report of extracted elements, relationships, and skip reasons
 *   - A completion alert summarizing the generated view
 *
 * This script reads a user-selected source view (typically Phase C: Information Systems Architecture), extracts
 * application-layer concepts and relationships, and creates a new Archi view with UML-style component-like diagram
 * notation, enhanced with AI-generated semantic classifications.
 *
 * Human-in-the-loop principle
 * ---------------------------
 *   The user explicitly selects the source View in Archi.
 *   Selection of a Phase C (Application Layer) view is the user's responsibility.
 *   The script does NOT validate view contents.
 *   AI analysis is optional and gracefully degrades if Ollama is unavailable.
 *
 * ArchiMate -> UML Visual Mappings
 * --------------------------------
 *
 *   Application Component       -> Component box
 *   Application Collaboration   -> Collaboration box (with stereotype)
 *   Application Interface       -> Interface box (with << >> notation)
 *   Application Service         -> Service box (with stereotype)
 *   Data Object                 -> Class box
 *   Business Object             -> BusinessEntity (with AI analysis)
 *   Business Process            -> BusinessProcess (with AI analysis)
 *
 * Supported Relationships
 * -----------------------
 *   Composition, Aggregation, Association, Realization, Specialization,
 *   Serving, Access, Flow, Triggering, Assignment, Influence
 *
 * A Note on UML Component Diagram
 * -------------------------------
 * UML diagram types, Component and Class diagrams are used to model the structural, implementation-oriented views of
 * object-oriented systems. The UML resemblance of the converted views, is primarily closer to the UML Component
 * Diagrams. The secondary resemblance is to the UML Class Diagrams, which is more stylistic and not structural identity
 * resemblance. They provide a practical means for visualizing, specifying, and documenting component-based systems, as
 * well as supporting the construction of executable systems through forward and reverse engineering. Component diagrams
 * can be viewed as a specialized form of class diagram that focuses on a system’s components, and are commonly used to
 * represent its static implementation view.
 *
 * Output
 * ------
 *   An ArchiMate View presenting the selected Application View in a UML-like Component Diagram-inspired
 *   visual style. It does not create native UML elements.
 *
 */

console.show();
console.clear();

// AI Integration: Attempt to load sendToAI.js for Ollama integration
let sendToAI = null;
let ollamaAvailable = false;
let ollamaFailureCount = 0;
let ollamaFailureThreshold = 3; // Disable AI after 3 consecutive failures

try {
    // Load sendToAI from current directory
    load(__DIR__ + "/sendToAI.js");
    
    // Small delay to ensure GraalJS has processed the load.
    // In GraalJS, load() executes synchronously but let's be defensive.
    if (typeof sendToAI !== "undefined" && typeof sendToAI === "function") {
        // Already available as local variable from load()
        ollamaAvailable = true;
    } else if (typeof globalThis !== "undefined" && typeof globalThis.sendToAI !== "undefined") {
        // Access from globalThis
        sendToAI = globalThis.sendToAI;
        ollamaAvailable = true;
    } else {
        // Try direct global access
        try {
            if (typeof eval("sendToAI") === "function") {
                sendToAI = eval("sendToAI");
                ollamaAvailable = true;
            }
        } catch (evalErr) {
            // Silent fail on eval
        }
    }
    
    if (ollamaAvailable) {
        console.log("[𐓙] Ollama integration loaded successfully.");
    } else {
        console.log("[𐓙] sendToAI.js loaded but function not accessible.");
        console.log("[𐓙] Proceeding without AI analysis (heuristic-only mode).");
    }
} catch (e) {
    console.log("[𐓙] sendToAI.js not found or error loading: " + e.message);
    console.log("[𐓙] Proceeding without AI analysis (heuristic-only mode).");
    ollamaAvailable = false;
}

/**
 * Analyze an element via Ollama to generate a UML-appropriate stereotype.
 * Returns a string (stereotype) or null if analysis unavailable.
 */
function analyzeElementWithAI(elementName, elementType, elementDoc) {
    if (!ollamaAvailable || !sendToAI) {
        return null;
    }

    try {
        const prompt = 
            "Given an ArchiMate element named '" + elementName + "' of type '" + elementType + "', " +
            "suggest a brief UML stereotype (1-2 words, lowercase). " +
            "Documentation: " + (elementDoc || "(none)") + "\n" +
            "Respond with ONLY the stereotype word(s), no explanation.";
        
        console.log("[𐓙] PROMPT for '" + elementName + "': " + prompt.substring(0, 60) + "...");
        
        const response = sendToAI(prompt);
        
        // Check for null response (API error or connection failure)
        if (response === null) {
            ollamaFailureCount++;
            if (ollamaFailureCount >= ollamaFailureThreshold) {
                console.warn("[𐓙] Ollama server unavailable after " + ollamaFailureCount + " attempts. Disabling AI mode.");
                ollamaAvailable = false;
            }
            return null;
        }
        
        // Reset failure counter on success
        ollamaFailureCount = 0;
        
        console.log("[𐓙] RESPONSE: '" + response + "'");
        
        if (response && response.length > 0 && response.length < 50) {
            const stereotype = sanitizeAiText(response.trim().toLowerCase());
            console.log("[𐓙] ✓ Extracted stereotype: '" + stereotype + "'");
            return stereotype;
        } else {
            console.log("[𐓙] ✗ Response too long or empty, skipping");
        }
    } catch (e) {
        console.log("[𐓙] ✗ Analysis error for '" + elementName + "': " + e);
    }

    return null;
}

/**
 * Generate an AI-enriched description for an element.
 * Returns a string or null if analysis unavailable.
 */
function generateElementDescriptionWithAI(elementName, elementType) {
    if (!ollamaAvailable || !sendToAI) {
        return null;
    }

    try {
        const prompt = 
            "Provide a brief (1-2 sentences) UML-oriented description of an ArchiMate " + 
            elementType + " named '" + elementName + "'. " +
            "Focus on its role and relationships in a system architecture.";
        
        console.log("[𐓙] DESCRIPTION PROMPT for '" + elementName + "': " + prompt.substring(0, 50) + "...");
        
        const response = sendToAI(prompt);
        
        // Check for null response (API error)
        if (response === null) {
            return null;
        }
        
        console.log("[𐓙] DESCRIPTION RESPONSE: '" + response.substring(0, 100) + "...'");
        
        if (response && response.length > 0 && response.length < 300) {
            return sanitizeAiText(response.trim());
        } else {
            console.log("[𐓙] ✗ Description response invalid or too long");
        }
    } catch (e) {
        console.log("[𐓙] ✗ Description generation error for '" + elementName + "': " + e);
    }

    return null;
}

function sanitizeAiText(raw) {
    if (raw === null || typeof raw === "undefined") {
        return "";
    }
    return String(raw)
        .replace(/[\u200E\u200F\uFEFF\u2060\u00A0]/g, "")
        .replace(/[\r\n\t]+/g, " ")
        .replace(/\s+/g, " ")
        .trim();
}

function normalizeAiStereotype(raw) {
    const cleaned = sanitizeAiText(raw)
        .replace(/[<>]/g, "")
        .replace(/^[^A-Za-z0-9]+|[^A-Za-z0-9]+$/g, "");

    if (!cleaned) {
        return null;
    }

    const words = cleaned.split(/\s+/).filter(function(word) {
        return word && word.length > 0;
    }).slice(0, 2);

    if (!words.length) {
        return null;
    }

    const normalized = words.join(" ").toLowerCase();
    if (normalized.length === 0 || normalized.length > 30) {
        return null;
    }

    return normalized;
}

function isStructuralContainerType(type) {
    if (!type) {
        return false;
    }
    const normalized = String(type).toLowerCase();
    return STRUCTURAL_CONTAINER_TYPES[normalized] || /group|package/i.test(normalized);
}

function isStructuralContainerObject(object) {
    if (!object) {
        return false;
    }

    const concept = object.concept;
    const conceptType = concept && concept.type;
    const objectType = object.type;

    if (isStructuralContainerType(conceptType) || isStructuralContainerType(objectType)) {
        return true;
    }

    if (object.parent && object.parent.concept && isStructuralContainerType(object.parent.concept.type)) {
        return true;
    }

    return false;
}

function getUmlConceptMetadata(concept) {
    // Retrieves semantic family and UML mapping information for an ArchiMate concept.
    // This function enables the "derivative-style UML representation" by mapping
    // ArchiMate concepts to UML-compatible semantic families and stereotypes.
    //
    // Parameters:
    //   concept: jArchi concept object with .type property
    //
    // Returns:
    //   Object with properties:
    //     - family: semantic family (component, interface, service, behavior, data, event)
    //     - umlKind: UML element kind (Component, Class, Interface, etc.)
    //     - stereotype: UML stereotype (lowercase, used in <<...>> notation)
    //     - packageGroup: logical grouping for organizational purposes
    //     - relationshipBias: preferred relationship semantic role (structural, dependency, etc.)
    //
    // The function follows the ArchiMate Layer principle: each ArchiMate concept is
    // mapped to a UML-equivalent visualization based on its semantic role, not its
    // syntactic ArchiMate name. This is the key innovation of derivative-style UML.
    
    if (!concept || !concept.type) {
        return {
            family: "artifact",
            umlKind: "Class",
            stereotype: "artifact",
            packageGroup: "General Elements",
            relationshipBias: "dependency"
        };
    }

    const type = String(concept.type).toLowerCase();
    const metadata = UML_CONCEPT_MAP[type] || {
        family: "artifact",
        umlKind: "Class",
        stereotype: "artifact",
        packageGroup: "General Elements",
        relationshipBias: "dependency"
    };

    return metadata;
}

function getRelationshipMetadata(relationship) {
    // Retrieves semantic role and styling information for an ArchiMate relationship.
    // This function maps ArchiMate relationship types to UML-compatible styles.
    //
    // Parameters:
    //   relationship: jArchi relationship concept object with .type property
    //
    // Returns:
    //   Object with properties:
    //     - umlStyle: UML connector style (composition, aggregation, association, etc.)
    //     - semanticRole: architectural semantic role (structural, dependency, behavior, etc.)
    //     - label: human-readable relationship label
    //
    // The semantic role determines the visual styling (color, line width) via the
    // RELATIONSHIP_ROLE_COLORS mapping, enabling visual differentiation of relationship
    // types in the generated UML-style diagram.
    
    if (!relationship || !relationship.type) {
        return {
            umlStyle: "association",
            semanticRole: "dependency",
            label: "association"
        };
    }

    const type = String(relationship.type).toLowerCase();
    return RELATIONSHIP_TYPES[type] || {
        umlStyle: "association",
        semanticRole: "dependency",
        label: "association"
    };
}

function applySemantticFamilyStyling(diagramObject, concept) {
    // Applies semantic family-based visual styling to a diagram element.
    // This is the visual manifestation of the "derivative-style UML" concept.
    //
    // Parameters:
    //   diagramObject: jArchi diagram model object created by view.add()
    //   concept: ArchiMate concept that the diagram object represents
    //
    // Behavior:
    //   1. Retrieves the semantic family of the concept via getUmlConceptMetadata()
    //   2. Looks up the color scheme from SEMANTIC_FAMILY_COLORS
    //   3. Applies fillColor, fontColor, and lineColor to the diagram object
    //   4. Silently continues on any styling errors (robustness)
    //
    // Note on jArchi Styling API:
    //   - jArchi diagram objects do not support direct CSS-style properties
    //   - fillColor, fontColor, lineColor are EMF/Archi color properties
    //   - Color values must be hex strings (#RRGGBB format)
    //   - Not all diagram object types support all color properties
    //   - Try-catch wrapping prevents styling failures from breaking diagram creation
    
    if (!diagramObject || !concept) {
        return;
    }

    const metadata = getUmlConceptMetadata(concept);
    const family = metadata && metadata.family ? metadata.family : "default";
    const colorScheme = SEMANTIC_FAMILY_COLORS[family] || SEMANTIC_FAMILY_COLORS["default"];

    try {
        // Apply colors to the diagram object
        // Each property is checked for existence before setting to avoid errors
        // on object types that don't support all color properties
        if (diagramObject.fillColor !== undefined) {
            diagramObject.fillColor = colorScheme.fillColor;
        }
        if (diagramObject.fontColor !== undefined) {
            diagramObject.fontColor = colorScheme.fontColor;
        }
        if (diagramObject.lineColor !== undefined) {
            diagramObject.lineColor = colorScheme.lineColor;
        }
    } catch (e) {
        // Silently ignore styling errors to not break element creation
        // The diagram will still be created even if colors cannot be applied
    }
}

function applyRelationshipRoleStyling(connectionLine, relationship) {
    // Applies semantic role-based visual styling to a diagram connection line.
    // This provides visual feedback about the semantic nature of relationships.
    //
    // Parameters:
    //   connectionLine: jArchi diagram connection object created by view.add(relationship, ...)
    //   relationship: ArchiMate relationship concept that the line represents
    //
    // Behavior:
    //   1. Retrieves the semantic role via getRelationshipMetadata()
    //   2. Looks up the line styling from RELATIONSHIP_ROLE_COLORS
    //   3. Applies lineColor and lineWidth to the connection object
    //   4. Silently continues on any styling errors (robustness)
    //
    // Note on jArchi Connection Styling API:
    //   - Connection objects support lineColor and lineWidth properties
    //   - lineColor values are hex strings (#RRGGBB format)
    //   - lineWidth is a numeric value (typically 1-3 for visibility)
    //   - Some Archi versions may not support lineWidth; try-catch handles this
    
    if (!connectionLine || !relationship) {
        return;
    }

    const metadata = getRelationshipMetadata(relationship);
    const role = metadata && metadata.semanticRole ? metadata.semanticRole : "default";
    const colorScheme = RELATIONSHIP_ROLE_COLORS[role] || RELATIONSHIP_ROLE_COLORS["default"];

    try {
        // Apply colors to the connection line
        if (connectionLine.lineColor !== undefined) {
            connectionLine.lineColor = colorScheme.lineColor;
        }
        if (connectionLine.lineWidth !== undefined) {
            connectionLine.lineWidth = colorScheme.lineWidth;
        }
    } catch (e) {
        // Silently ignore styling errors to not break relationship creation
        // The connection will still be created even if styles cannot be applied
    }
}

// ArchiMate types recognized for UML view generation
// ============================================================================
// Includes all Application Layer elements and related Business/Data types
//
// SEMANTIC FAMILY CLASSIFICATION (per Open Group white paper):
// This mapping implements the core principle of "derivative-style UML representation":
// instead of performing syntactic 1:1 type conversion, we classify ArchiMate concepts
// by their semantic role within the architecture. This allows:
//
//   1. UML-FRIENDLY VISUALIZATION: ArchiMate concepts are grouped by UML-compatible
//      semantic roles (Component, Interface, Service, Behavior, Data, Event) rather
//      than by their specific ArchiMate type.
//
//   2. SEMANTIC PRESERVATION: The mapping preserves ArchiMate semantics while making
//      them visible in a UML-friendly notation, enabling interoperability between
//      ArchiMate and UML tooling communities.
//
//   3. RELATIONSHIP SEMANTICS: Each type includes a "relationshipBias" that indicates
//      the preferred semantic role for this element's relationships (structural,
//      dependency, behavioral, etc.).
//
// LAYERING PRINCIPLE:
// ArchiMate organizes the enterprise architecture into layers:
//   - Business Layer: business processes, actors, capabilities
//   - Application Layer: application components, services, interfaces
//   - Technology Layer: infrastructure, platforms, devices
//
// This script focuses on Application Layer and related Business/Data concepts because:
//   - Application components are most directly analogous to UML components
//   - Services and interfaces have direct UML equivalents
//   - Business processes provide behavioral context
//   - Data objects represent information assets
//
// Each ArchiMate concept in jArchi has:
//   - .type: string identifier (e.g., "application-component")
//   - .name: human-readable label
//   - .id: unique identifier within the model
//   - .documentation: optional description text
//   - .properties: optional key-value pairs
//   - .parent: reference to containing element (if any)
//
// UML_CONCEPT_MAP ENTRY STRUCTURE:
// Each entry defines how an ArchiMate type maps to UML representation:
//   - family: semantic family (component, interface, service, behavior, data, event)
//   - umlKind: UML element kind (used for documentation/notation)
//   - stereotype: UML stereotype label (lowercase, used in <<...>> notation)
//   - packageGroup: logical grouping for organizational purposes
//   - relationshipBias: semantic role for relationships (structural, dependency, behavior, trigger, realization, generalization)
// ============================================================================
const UML_CONCEPT_MAP = {
    // APPLICATION LAYER - COMPONENTS: Structural building blocks
    "application-component": {
        family: "component",
        umlKind: "Component",
        stereotype: "component",
        packageGroup: "Application Components",
        relationshipBias: "structural"
    },
    "application-collaboration": {
        family: "component",
        umlKind: "Collaboration",
        stereotype: "collaboration",
        packageGroup: "Application Components",
        relationshipBias: "structural"
    },
    
    // APPLICATION LAYER - INTERFACES: Contract/boundary elements
    "application-interface": {
        family: "interface",
        umlKind: "Interface",
        stereotype: "interface",
        packageGroup: "Application Interfaces",
        relationshipBias: "dependency"
    },
    
    // APPLICATION LAYER - SERVICES: Behavioral capabilities
    "application-service": {
        family: "service",
        umlKind: "Service",
        stereotype: "service",
        packageGroup: "Application Services",
        relationshipBias: "dependency"
    },
    
    // APPLICATION LAYER - BEHAVIOR: Functions, processes, interactions
    "application-function": {
        family: "behavior",
        umlKind: "Function",
        stereotype: "function",
        packageGroup: "Application Behavior",
        relationshipBias: "behavior"
    },
    "application-process": {
        family: "behavior",
        umlKind: "Process",
        stereotype: "process",
        packageGroup: "Application Behavior",
        relationshipBias: "behavior"
    },
    "application-interaction": {
        family: "behavior",
        umlKind: "Interaction",
        stereotype: "interaction",
        packageGroup: "Application Behavior",
        relationshipBias: "behavior"
    },
    
    // APPLICATION LAYER - EVENTS: Stimulus/response elements
    "application-event": {
        family: "event",
        umlKind: "Event",
        stereotype: "event",
        packageGroup: "Application Events",
        relationshipBias: "trigger"
    },
    
    // APPLICATION LAYER - INFORMATION: Application-level data objects
    "application-object": {
        family: "artifact",
        umlKind: "Object",
        stereotype: "object",
        packageGroup: "Application Objects",
        relationshipBias: "dependency"
    },
    "data-object": {
        family: "data",
        umlKind: "Class",
        stereotype: "dataObject",
        packageGroup: "Data Objects",
        relationshipBias: "structural"
    },
    "business-object": {
        family: "data",
        umlKind: "BusinessEntity",
        stereotype: "businessObject",
        packageGroup: "Business Objects",
        relationshipBias: "structural"
    },
    "business-process": {
        family: "behavior",
        umlKind: "BusinessProcess",
        stereotype: "businessProcess",
        packageGroup: "Business Processes",
        relationshipBias: "behavior"
    }
};

// Supported relationship types for diagram connections
// ============================================================================
// ArchiMate defines multiple relationship types that represent different kinds
// of dependencies and connections between concepts. This mapping classifies them
// by their semantic role for UML-style visualization.
//
// RELATIONSHIP SEMANTICS IN ARCHIMATE:
// ArchiMate relationships fall into three main categories:
//
//   1. STRUCTURAL RELATIONSHIPS: Composition, Aggregation
//      - Represent "part-of" or containment relationships
//      - Composition: Strong ownership (part cannot exist without whole)
//      - Aggregation: Weak containment (part can exist independently)
//      - UML Analogy: Component composition and aggregation
//      - Visual: Solid, thick lines (high importance)
//
//   2. DEPENDENCY RELATIONSHIPS: Association, Serving, Access, Assignment, Influence
//      - Represent various forms of dependency or use relationships
//      - Association: General connection or usage relationship
//      - Serving: One element provides capability to another
//      - Access: One element accesses data/resources of another
//      - Assignment: Implementation or allocation relationship
//      - Influence: One element affects another (weaker connection)
//      - UML Analogy: Dependencies, associations, generalizations
//      - Visual: Dashed or standard lines (lower importance than structural)
//
//   3. BEHAVIORAL RELATIONSHIPS: Flow, Triggering
//      - Represent process flow and causality
//      - Flow: Sequential or data flow between elements
//      - Triggering: One element causes another to execute
//      - UML Analogy: Message sequences, temporal ordering
//      - Visual: Green lines (activity/process indicator)
//
//   4. ABSTRACTION RELATIONSHIPS: Realization, Generalization/Specialization
//      - Represent abstraction hierarchies and implementation
//      - Realization: Implementation of an interface/specification
//      - Generalization/Specialization: Type hierarchy
//      - UML Analogy: Implementation, inheritance
//      - Visual: Colored lines (purple for realization, red for generalization)
//
// ArchiMate RELATIONSHIP CREATION IN jArchi:
// To create a diagram relationship in jArchi, you use:
//   const connectionLine = view.add(relationship, sourceObject, targetObject);
// where:
//   - relationship: jArchi relationship concept object
//   - sourceObject: jArchi diagram object representing the source concept
//   - targetObject: jArchi diagram object representing the target concept
//   - returns: jArchi diagram connection object (or null if failed)
//
// RELATIONSHIP ENTRY STRUCTURE:
//   - umlStyle: UML equivalent style notation
//   - semanticRole: architectural semantic role used for color/style mapping
//   - label: human-readable relationship label
// ============================================================================
const RELATIONSHIP_TYPES = {
    // ========== STRUCTURAL RELATIONSHIPS: "Part-of" semantics ==========
    "composition-relationship": {
        umlStyle: "composition",
        semanticRole: "structural",      // Blue lines, thick (important)
        label: "composition"              // Strong ownership
    },
    "aggregation-relationship": {
        umlStyle: "aggregation",
        semanticRole: "structural",      // Blue lines, thick
        label: "aggregation"              // Weak containment
    },
    
    // ========== DEPENDENCY RELATIONSHIPS: Usage/connection semantics ==========
    "association-relationship": {
        umlStyle: "association",
        semanticRole: "dependency",      // Orange lines (dependency indicator)
        label: "association"              // General connection/usage
    },
    "serving-relationship": {
        umlStyle: "serving",
        semanticRole: "dependency",      // Orange lines
        label: "serving"                  // Capability provision (e.g., service to client)
    },
    "access-relationship": {
        umlStyle: "access",
        semanticRole: "dependency",      // Orange lines
        label: "access"                   // Data/resource access (e.g., component accesses data)
    },
    "assignment-relationship": {
        umlStyle: "assignment",
        semanticRole: "dependency",      // Orange lines
        label: "assignment"               // Implementation/allocation (e.g., app implements service)
    },
    "influence-relationship": {
        umlStyle: "influence",
        semanticRole: "dependency",      // Orange lines
        label: "influence"                // Weaker influence relationship
    },
    
    // ========== BEHAVIORAL RELATIONSHIPS: Process flow semantics ==========
    "flow-relationship": {
        umlStyle: "flow",
        semanticRole: "behavior",        // Green lines (activity/flow indicator)
        label: "flow"                     // Sequential or data flow
    },
    "triggering-relationship": {
        umlStyle: "triggering",
        semanticRole: "behavior",        // Green lines
        label: "triggering"               // Causality (A triggers B)
    },
    
    // ========== ABSTRACTION RELATIONSHIPS: Type hierarchy semantics ==========
    "realization-relationship": {
        umlStyle: "realization",
        semanticRole: "realization",     // Purple lines (implementation indicator)
        label: "realization"              // Implementation of interface/specification
    },
    "specialization-relationship": {
        umlStyle: "generalization",
        semanticRole: "generalization",  // Red lines (inheritance/type indicator)
        label: "generalization"           // Type hierarchy (subtype-of)
    }
};

// Grid layout parameters for diagram element positioning
// ============================================================================
// When creating a new UML-style view, elements need to be positioned in the
// diagram. This script uses a simple grid-based layout algorithm:
//
//   1. Elements are placed in a grid with GRID_SPACING_X and GRID_SPACING_Y
//   2. Elements start at (START_X, START_Y)
//   3. Each element occupies ELEMENT_WIDTH x ELEMENT_HEIGHT pixels
//   4. Elements are placed left-to-right, top-to-bottom (max 4 columns per row)
//   5. After 4 columns, wrap to the next row
//
// This provides a clean, organized layout that is easy to read and modify.
// The positions are set during view creation via:
//   view.add(concept, x, y, width, height);
//
// Note on Archi Coordinates:
//   - Archi uses pixel coordinates for diagram element positioning
//   - (0, 0) is typically the top-left corner
//   - Y increases downward
//   - Z-order (layering) is determined by creation order
// ============================================================================
const GRID_SPACING_X = 250;      // Horizontal spacing between elements (pixels)
const GRID_SPACING_Y = 200;      // Vertical spacing between rows (pixels)
const ELEMENT_WIDTH = 180;       // Width of each element box (pixels)
const ELEMENT_HEIGHT = 100;      // Height of each element box (pixels)
const START_X = 50;              // Starting X coordinate (pixels from left)
const START_Y = 50;              // Starting Y coordinate (pixels from top)

// Semantic family color scheme for UML-style visualization
// ============================================================================
// This color scheme implements the "derivative-style UML representation" concept
// from the Open Group's "How to Use the ArchiMate Language" white paper.
//
// Instead of converting ArchiMate concepts to "real UML" (which is impossible),
// we create a UML-like notation view that is a derived representation of the
// ArchiMate model, preserving source semantics while offering UML-friendly
// visual structure.
//
// The semantic families group ArchiMate types by their architectural role:
//  - COMPONENT: Structural building blocks (components, collaborations, services)
//  - INTERFACE: Contract/boundary elements that connect components
//  - SERVICE: Behavioral capabilities and service definitions
//  - BEHAVIOR: Business processes and application functions
//  - DATA: Information and data objects
//  - EVENT: Stimulus/response elements
//  - CONTAINER: Structural grouping containers
//
// Each family has three colors:
//  - fillColor: Background color of the element box
//  - fontColor: Text color for element name and text
//  - lineColor: Border/outline color of the element
//
// Color psychology applied:
//  - Blue (component): Trust, stability, structure
//  - Purple (interface): Abstraction, boundaries
//  - Green (service): Growth, capability, positive action
//  - Orange (behavior): Energy, processes, activity
//  - Red (data): Information, importance, critical assets
//  - Magenta (event): Stimulus, triggers, communication
// ============================================================================
const SEMANTIC_FAMILY_COLORS = {
    "component": {
        fillColor: "#E8F4F8",      // Light cyan - professional, structural
        fontColor: "#004080",      // Dark blue - readable, authoritative
        lineColor: "#0066CC"       // Blue - represents structural integrity
    },
    "interface": {
        fillColor: "#F0E8F8",      // Light purple - represents boundaries
        fontColor: "#330066",      // Dark purple - contract-oriented
        lineColor: "#9933CC"       // Purple - abstraction layer indicator
    },
    "service": {
        fillColor: "#E8F8E8",      // Light green - growth, capability
        fontColor: "#004000",      // Dark green - positive, action-oriented
        lineColor: "#00AA00"       // Green - service/capability indicator
    },
    "behavior": {
        fillColor: "#FFF4E6",      // Light orange - process, activity
        fontColor: "#804000",      // Dark orange-brown - warm, process-oriented
        lineColor: "#FF9900"       // Orange - workflow, activity indicator
    },
    "data": {
        fillColor: "#FFE8E8",      // Light red - information, critical
        fontColor: "#800000",      // Dark red - importance indicator
        lineColor: "#CC0000"       // Red - data/information asset
    },
    "event": {
        fillColor: "#F8E8F8",      // Light magenta - stimulus, trigger
        fontColor: "#660066",      // Dark magenta - significant, notable
        lineColor: "#DD00DD"       // Magenta - event/trigger indicator
    },
    "container": {
        fillColor: "#F0F0F0",      // Light gray - grouping, structural
        fontColor: "#333333",      // Dark gray - neutral, background
        lineColor: "#666666"       // Gray - container/grouping indicator
    },
    "default": {
        fillColor: "#F5F5F5",      // Very light gray - fallback/unknown
        fontColor: "#555555",      // Medium gray - neutral
        lineColor: "#999999"       // Light gray border - unclassified
    }
};

// Semantic role-based styling for relationships
// ============================================================================
// Relationships (connections between elements) are classified by their semantic
// role in the architecture, following the Open Group UML/ArchiMate interoperability
// guidelines. This allows visual differentiation of relationship types:
//
// STRUCTURAL: Composition and aggregation relationships
//   - Indicates "part-of" or hierarchical containment
//   - Blue solid lines represent solid, permanent structural bonds
//
// DEPENDENCY: Service, access, assignment, and influence relationships
//   - Indicates that one element depends on or influences another
//   - Orange dashed/thin lines represent less rigid dependencies
//
// BEHAVIORAL: Flow and triggering relationships
//   - Indicates process flow, sequences, or causality
//   - Green lines represent activity flow and process choreography
//
// REALIZATION: Realization relationships
//   - Indicates implementation of an interface or specification
//   - Purple lines represent implementation bonds
//
// GENERALIZATION: Specialization/inheritance relationships
//   - Indicates type hierarchy or classification
//   - Red lines represent inheritance and type relationships
// ============================================================================
const RELATIONSHIP_ROLE_COLORS = {
    "structural": {
        lineColor: "#0066CC",      // Blue - structural integrity
        lineWidth: 2               // Thicker line for important structural bonds
    },
    "dependency": {
        lineColor: "#FF9900",      // Orange - dependency indicator
        lineWidth: 2               // Standard width for dependency relationships
    },
    "behavior": {
        lineColor: "#00AA00",      // Green - process flow indicator
        lineWidth: 2               // Standard width for behavioral flow
    },
    "realization": {
        lineColor: "#9933CC",      // Purple - implementation indicator
        lineWidth: 2               // Standard width for realization relationships
    },
    "generalization": {
        lineColor: "#CC0000",      // Red - inheritance/type relationship
        lineWidth: 2               // Standard width for generalization
    },
    "default": {
        lineColor: "#666666",      // Gray - unknown relationship type
        lineWidth: 1               // Thinner line for unclassified relationships
    }
};

function collectVisualObjects(node, list, seen) {
    if (!node || !list) {
        return;
    }

    const nodeId = (node && node.id) || (node && node.concept && node.concept.id);
    if (nodeId && seen[nodeId]) {
        return;
    }
    if (nodeId) {
        seen[nodeId] = true;
    }

    list.push(node);

    try {
        const children = $(node).children();
        if (children && children.length) {
            children.each(function(child) {
                collectVisualObjects(child, list, seen);
            });
        }
    } catch (e) {
        // Ignore traversal errors and continue with already collected objects.
    }
}

const STRUCTURAL_CONTAINER_TYPES = {
    "grouping": true,
    "group": true,
    "diagram-model-group": true
};


/* ============================================================================
 * MAIN EXECUTION FLOW
 *
 * This section orchestrates the complete ArchiMate-to-UML transformation:
 *   1. Obtain user-selected view
 *   2. Extract visual objects (concepts and relationships) from source view
 *   3. Filter for supported element types
 *   4. Create new UML-style view in the model
 *   5. Add elements to new view with AI enrichment (optional)
 *   6. Add relationships to new view
 *   7. Report results
 *
 * jArchi API NOTES:
 *   - $(selection): jArchi collection API; selects items from current context
 *   - $(selection).filter("view"): filters to view elements only
 *   - $(view).children(): recursively gets child elements
 *   - $(view).children("connection"): explicitly gets diagram connections
 *   - view.add(concept, x, y, width, height): creates diagram element
 *   - view.add(relationship, sourceObject, targetObject): creates diagram connection
 *   - Each collection method returns an object with .length and .each() methods
 *
 * GraalJS RUNTIME NOTES:
 *   - Global object is 'globalThis' in GraalJS
 *   - No async/await or Promises (synchronous only)
 *   - Java interop via Java.type() for Java class access
 *   - String interpolation via + operator only (no template literals in some versions)
 * ============================================================================ */

/* ============================================================================
 * 1. Obtain the user-selected View
 * ============================================================================
 * Retrieving the source view is the first step. The user must select a view
 * in Archi's Model Tree before running this script. The jArchi selection
 * global captures the current selection in the UI.
 *
 * jArchi SELECTION API:
 *   - selection: global variable set by jArchi on script execution
 *   - Contains the currently selected elements in Archi's UI
 *   - $(selection): converts selection to a jArchi collection
 *   - .filter("view"): keeps only view elements (diagram models)
 *   - .first(): returns the first element or null
 *
 * For users, the typical workflow is:
 *   1. Open Archi and load a model
 *   2. Locate a view in the Model Tree (e.g., "Phase C: Information Systems Architecture")
 *   3. Right-click and run this script
 *   4. The script captures that selected view
 * ============================================================================ */

const selectedView = $(selection).filter("view").first();

if (!selectedView) {
    window.alert(
        "Please select an ArchiMate View (e.g., Phase C: Information Systems)\n" +
        "in Archi's Models Tree and run the script again."
    );
    throw new Error("No View selected.");
}

const sourceView = selectedView;
console.log("Application View → UML Notation");
console.log("Source View: " + sourceView.name);

/* ============================================================================
 * 2. Extract visual objects and relationships from the source View
 * ============================================================================
 * A view is a diagram consisting of visual objects (diagram elements) and
 * connections (diagram relationships). We need to extract both to create
 * the new UML-style view.
 *
 * jArchi VIEW STRUCTURE:
 * In jArchi, a view is a container of:
 *   - diagram-model-object: visual representation of an ArchiMate concept
 *     - Has .concept property pointing to the underlying ArchiMate model
 *     - Has .source and .target only if it's a connection
 *     - Has position properties (x, y) and size (width, height)
 *
 *   - diagram-model-connection: visual representation of a relationship
 *     - Has .concept property pointing to the underlying relationship
 *     - Has .source: diagram-model-object at starting end
 *     - Has .target: diagram-model-object at ending end
 *
 * COLLECTION TRAVERSAL IN jArchi:
 *   - $(node).children(): recursively collects all descendants
 *   - $(view).children("connection"): specifically gets connection type descendants
 *   - The recursion handles nested groups and complex hierarchies
 *   - .each(function(item) {...}): iterates collection items
 * ============================================================================ */

const visualObjects = [];
const seenObjects = {};
collectVisualObjects(sourceView, visualObjects, seenObjects);

// Also explicitly collect connections from the view
// This is a two-step approach:
//   1. collectVisualObjects traverses the tree to find visual objects
//   2. We then explicitly query for "connection" type objects
// This ensures we capture all connections even if hierarchy is unusual
const connections_raw = [];
try {
    // jArchi COLLECTION API FOR CONNECTIONS:
    // $(sourceView).children("connection"): queries direct and nested connections
    // The .each() method is the primary iteration mechanism in jArchi collections
    $(sourceView).children("connection").each(function(conn) {
        if (conn && conn.source && conn.target) {
            connections_raw.push(conn);
        }
    });
} catch (e) {
    // Connections may not exist in this view; silently continue
    // The view may contain only elements with no relationships
}

/* ============================================================================
 * Filter for supported element types and excluded structural containers
 * ============================================================================
 * ArchiMate views can contain many element types. We only want:
 *   - Supported application layer elements (see UML_CONCEPT_MAP)
 *   - Element types that exist in RELATIONSHIP_TYPES for relationships
 *
 * We explicitly exclude:
 *   - Structural containers (group, grouping, diagram-model-group)
 *     These are ArchiMate grouping elements used for organization
 *     We don't want them in the UML view as they represent pure containment
 *     Elements that are CHILDREN of containers are still included individually
 *   - Container-type concepts (family: "container")
 *     This includes grouping and other pure structural elements
 *
 * The filtering happens in three steps:
 *   1. isStructuralContainerObject: checks the visual object type
 *   2. isStructuralContainerType: checks the concept type
 *   3. Check if object's parent is a structural container
 ============================================================================ */

const flattenedVisualObjects = [];
visualObjects.forEach(function(object) {
    if (!object || isStructuralContainerObject(object)) {
        return;
    }

    const concept = object.concept;
    const conceptType = concept && concept.type;
    const objectType = object.type;

    // Filter out both the visual object type and the concept type
    if (isStructuralContainerType(conceptType) || isStructuralContainerType(objectType)) {
        return;
    }

    // Also filter out children of containers to avoid orphans
    if (object.parent && object.parent.concept && isStructuralContainerType(object.parent.concept.type)) {
        return;
    }

    flattenedVisualObjects.push(object);
});

// Add explicitly collected connections to visual objects for processing
// This includes them in the main extraction loop below
connections_raw.forEach(function(conn) {
    flattenedVisualObjects.push(conn);
});

const concepts = {};
const connections = [];
const foundTypes = {};
const skippedTypes = {};

flattenedVisualObjects.forEach(function(object) {
    const concept = object.concept;

    // Handle diagram connections/relationships (with source and target)
    if (object.source && object.target && object.concept) {
        connections.push(object);
        return;
    }

    // Only process concept elements (skip relationship concepts)
    if (concept) {
        const type = concept.type;
        
        // Skip relationship types - they should only be added via connections
        if (RELATIONSHIP_TYPES[type]) {
            skippedTypes[type] = (skippedTypes[type] || 0) + 1;
            return;
        }
        
        const metadata = getUmlConceptMetadata(concept);

        if (metadata && metadata.family !== "container") {
            concepts[concept.id] = concept;
            foundTypes[type] = (foundTypes[type] || 0) + 1;
            return;
        }

        skippedTypes[type] = (skippedTypes[type] || 0) + 1;
    }
});

console.log("Elements extracted: " + Object.keys(concepts).length);
console.log("Relationships extracted: " + connections.length);

// Report recognized vs. unrecognized element types
if (Object.keys(foundTypes).length > 0) {
    console.log("\nRecognized element types:");
    Object.keys(foundTypes).forEach(function(type) {
        console.log("  + " + type + ": " + foundTypes[type]);
    });
}

if (Object.keys(skippedTypes).length > 0) {
    console.log("\nUnrecognized element types (not included):");
    Object.keys(skippedTypes).forEach(function(type) {
        console.log("  - " + type + ": " + skippedTypes[type]);
    });
}

// 3. Create a new UML-notation View in the model

const newViewName = sourceView.name + " (UML)";

// Create new view (jArchi API: createArchimateView(name) or createArchimateView(name, folder))
let newView = model.createArchimateView(newViewName);

if (!newView) {
    throw new Error("Failed to create new Archi view.");
}

// Add documentation to the view
let viewDocumentation = "UML-notation view generated from: " + sourceView.name;
if (ollamaAvailable) {
    viewDocumentation += "\n\n[AI-ENRICHED] Elements contain AI-generated semantic stereotypes and descriptions from Ollama analysis.";
    viewDocumentation += "\nLook for [AI] prefix in element documentation to distinguish AI-generated from heuristic classifications.";
} else {
    viewDocumentation += "\n\n[HEURISTIC-ONLY] Ollama not available. Elements use standard heuristic-based stereotypes.";
}
newView.documentation = viewDocumentation;

console.log("Created new view: " + newViewName);

// 4. Create diagram objects in the new view (with AI enrichment)

const diagramObjectMap = {}; // Maps concept.id -> diagramObject

let gridRow = 0;
let gridCol = 0;
let elementsAdded = 0;
let aiAnalysisCount = 0;

Object.keys(concepts).forEach(function(conceptId) {
    const concept = concepts[conceptId];
    const typeInfo = getUmlConceptMetadata(concept);

    if (ollamaAvailable) {
        console.log("[𐓙] Analyzing: " + concept.name);

        const aiStereotype = analyzeElementWithAI(
            concept.name,
            concept.type,
            concept.documentation || ""
        );

        if (aiStereotype) {
            const aiMarkedStereotype = "[AI] " + aiStereotype;
            if (concept.documentation) {
                concept.documentation = "<<" + aiMarkedStereotype + ">>\n" + concept.documentation;
            } else {
                concept.documentation = "<<" + aiMarkedStereotype + ">>";
            }
            aiAnalysisCount++;
            console.log("[𐓙] ✓ Applied 𐓙 stereotype: <<" + aiMarkedStereotype + ">>");
        }

        const aiDescription = generateElementDescriptionWithAI(
            concept.name,
            concept.type
        );
        if (aiDescription && !concept.documentation) {
            console.log("[𐓙] 📝 Applied AI description: " + aiDescription.substring(0, 60) + "...");
            concept.documentation = "[AI-Generated Description]\n" + aiDescription;
        }
    } else if (typeInfo && typeInfo.stereotype) {
        const heurStereotype = "[HEUR] " + typeInfo.stereotype;
        concept.documentation = "<<" + heurStereotype + ">>\n" +
            (concept.documentation || "");
    }

    const x = START_X + gridCol * GRID_SPACING_X;
    const y = START_Y + gridRow * GRID_SPACING_Y;

    const diagramObject = newView.add(
        concept,
        x,
        y,
        ELEMENT_WIDTH,
        ELEMENT_HEIGHT
    );

    if (diagramObject) {
        // Apply semantic family-based styling
        applySemantticFamilyStyling(diagramObject, concept);
        
        diagramObjectMap[conceptId] = diagramObject;
        elementsAdded++;
        gridCol++;
        if (gridCol > 3) {
            gridCol = 0;
            gridRow++;
        }
    }
});

console.log("Diagram objects created: " + elementsAdded);
if (aiAnalysisCount > 0) {
    console.log("✓ Elements enriched with AI analysis: " + aiAnalysisCount);
} else if (ollamaAvailable) {
    console.log("⚠ Ollama available but no AI stereotypes were generated");
}

//5. Create diagram connections for relationships

let connectionsAdded = 0;
let skippedConnections = 0;
const skipReasons = {};

connections.forEach(function(connection) {
    const relationship = connection.concept;

    if (!relationship) {
        skippedConnections++;
        skipReasons["no_relationship"] = (skipReasons["no_relationship"] || 0) + 1;
        return;
    }

    const sourceVisual = connection.source;
    const targetVisual = connection.target;

    if (!sourceVisual || !targetVisual) {
        skippedConnections++;
        skipReasons["missing_visual_endpoint"] = (skipReasons["missing_visual_endpoint"] || 0) + 1;
        return;
    }

    const sourceConcept = sourceVisual.concept;
    const targetConcept = targetVisual.concept;

    if (!sourceConcept || !targetConcept) {
        skippedConnections++;
        skipReasons["missing_concept_endpoint"] = (skipReasons["missing_concept_endpoint"] || 0) + 1;
        return;
    }

    if (!diagramObjectMap[sourceConcept.id]) {
        skippedConnections++;
        skipReasons["source_not_in_view"] = (skipReasons["source_not_in_view"] || 0) + 1;
        return;
    }

    if (!diagramObjectMap[targetConcept.id]) {
        skippedConnections++;
        skipReasons["target_not_in_view"] = (skipReasons["target_not_in_view"] || 0) + 1;
        return;
    }

    if (!RELATIONSHIP_TYPES[relationship.type]) {
        skippedConnections++;
        skipReasons["unsupported_relationship_type:" + relationship.type] =
            (skipReasons["unsupported_relationship_type:" + relationship.type] || 0) + 1;
        return;
    }

    const sourceObject = diagramObjectMap[sourceConcept.id];
    const targetObject = diagramObjectMap[targetConcept.id];

    try {
        const connectionLine = newView.add(relationship, sourceObject, targetObject);
        if (connectionLine) {
            // Apply semantic role-based styling to the relationship line
            applyRelationshipRoleStyling(connectionLine, relationship);
            
            connectionsAdded++;
        } else {
            skippedConnections++;
            skipReasons["add_failed"] = (skipReasons["add_failed"] || 0) + 1;
        }
    } catch (e) {
        console.log("Failed to add connection: " + e);
        skippedConnections++;
        skipReasons["exception"] = (skipReasons["exception"] || 0) + 1;
    }
});

console.log("Connections created: " + connectionsAdded);
console.log("Connections skipped: " + skippedConnections);

if (skippedConnections > 0 && Object.keys(skipReasons).length > 0) {
    console.log("\nSkip reasons:");
    Object.keys(skipReasons).forEach(function(reason) {
        console.log("  - " + reason + ": " + skipReasons[reason]);
    });
}


// 6. Report summary and completion

console.log("");
console.log("==============================================");
console.log("UML View Generation Completed");
console.log("==============================================");
console.log("Source View: " + sourceView.name);
console.log("Target View: " + newViewName);
console.log("Elements: " + elementsAdded);
console.log("Connections: " + connectionsAdded);

if (ollamaAvailable && aiAnalysisCount > 0) {
    console.log("✓ Elements enriched with AI: " + aiAnalysisCount + " / " + elementsAdded);
    console.log("[𐓙] Ollama analysis completed successfully.");
    console.log("[𐓙] Stereotypes marked with [𐓙] prefix in element documentation.");
} else if (!ollamaAvailable) {
    console.log("⚠ Ollama not available - used heuristic-only mode.");
    console.log("   Stereotypes marked with [HEUR] prefix in element documentation.");
} else if (ollamaAvailable && aiAnalysisCount === 0) {
    console.log("⚠ Ollama available but no elements were analyzed (check Ollama server).");
}

console.log("");
console.log("The new UML-notation view is now visible in");
console.log("Archi's Models panel. You can edit, rearrange,");
console.log("and publish this view directly.");
console.log("");
console.log("VERIFICATION: Check element documentation for:");
console.log("  • [𐓙] prefix   = AI-generated from Ollama");
console.log("  • [HEUR] prefix = Heuristic-only (Ollama unavailable)");

const alertMsg = 
    "UML View Generation Completed\n\n" +
    "Source: " + sourceView.name + "\n" +
    "New View: " + newViewName + "\n\n" +
    "Elements: " + elementsAdded + "\n" +
    "Connections: " + connectionsAdded + "\n" +
    (ollamaAvailable && aiAnalysisCount > 0 ? 
        "✓ AI-Enriched Elements: " + aiAnalysisCount + " / " + elementsAdded + "\n\n" +
        "Check element documentation for [𐓙] prefix to verify\n" +
        "AI-generated stereotypes vs. heuristic ones.\n\n" :
        (ollamaAvailable ? 
            "⚠ Ollama available but check if server is running\n\n" :
            "Ollama not available - heuristic-only mode used\n\n")) +
    "The view is now in your model and can be\n" +
    "edited and published directly from Archi.";

window.alert(alertMsg);



